= Introduction {#sec:intro}

== Purpose

This document specifies the design of the integration between the ACME checkout
service and the \gateway payment gateway.\footnote{\gateway API version 2024-11 is
assumed throughout.} It is intended for developers, testers and the security
review board. The vendor documentation is at <https://docs.payflow.example/v2024-11>.

== Scope

The integration covers card authorisation, capture, refunds (@sec:refunds) and
asynchronous notifications (@sec:webhooks). Recurring billing is _out of scope_
and will be addressed in a separate specification.

== Definitions

@tbl:terms lists the terms used in this specification.

\caption[id=tbl:terms]{Terms and definitions}
\table[header-rows=1, widths=(4cm, 1fr)]{
  | Term            | Definition                                                     |
  | Authorisation   | A hold placed on the cardholder's funds.                        |
  | Capture         | Settlement of a previously authorised amount.                   |
  | Idempotency key | A client-generated UUID that makes a request safe to retry.     |
}

= Architecture {#sec:arch}

\figure[id=fig:context, width=15cm]{
  \image[alt={System context: the web shop sends orders to the checkout API, which calls
    PayFlow through the payment adapter; PayFlow sends webhooks to the webhook
    receiver.}]{img/context-diagram.png}
  \caption{System context}
}

@fig:context shows how the payment adapter sits between ACME and the gateway.

The checkout service never handles raw card numbers: card data is tokenised in the
browser by the \gateway SDK, and only tokens reach ACME systems.

== Components

- *Checkout API*: receives orders from the web shop and owns the order state.
- *Payment Adapter*: translates domain commands into \gateway API calls.
  It is the only component holding gateway credentials.
- *Webhook Receiver*: verifies and persists gateway events.

== Error Handling

Gateway errors are mapped to adapter behaviour in the appendix (@app:errors).

= Functional Requirements {#sec:reqs}

== Authorisation

\req{The adapter shall send amounts in minor currency units.}

\req{Every authorisation request shall carry an idempotency key.}

\req[priority=Should]{The adapter should time out after 10~s and retry at most twice,
reusing the idempotency key.}

== Refunds {#sec:refunds}

\req{Partial refunds shall be supported up to the captured amount.}

\warning{Refunds older than 180 days are rejected by \gateway and must be handled
manually by Finance.}

== Webhooks {#sec:webhooks}

Events are signed with HMAC-SHA256. The receiver verifies the signature before
parsing the body (@lst:verify):

```python {#lst:verify, caption="Webhook signature verification"}
def verify(payload: bytes, header: str, secret: bytes) -> bool:
    expected = hmac.new(secret, payload, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()
    return hmac.compare_digest(expected, header)
```

\req{The receiver shall reject events whose signature does not verify, and log them.}

\tip[title="Testing"]{\gateway's sandbox can replay any event from its dashboard,
which makes signature failures easy to reproduce.}

